@BurleyBob0010  Willful ignorance isn't an argument. "Doing your own research" is pointless without due diligence. You just spout absurd speculation without any specific evidence for your "super advanced ancient machines" - because you childishly speculate about things you know nothing about and think/insist rational people must take you seriously. And you likely denounce trained professionals ( like an ignorant contumelious putz) that are required to temper their statements with actual facts - and know more than you ever will on the subject.

The World of Antiquity YouTube channel has various videos on why you're full of shite. Search for "Things Alternative Ancient History Purveyors Can't Explain" - or - "Historian Reacts to Evidence for Ancient High Technology in Egypt"  - and that's just from one source, multiple sources are available

... grow up

@ipelengmanthaaa49 "1000 centuries" is 100,000 years. Nothing built by humans is anywhere near that old. The oldest monumental structure we know of, Gobekli Tepe, is about 11,600 years old - that is 116 centuries, not 1000. The Great Pyramid is about 4,600 years old. You are off by a factor of about ten before you even get to your argument.

And "still standing" is not a measure of how advanced anything is. A pyramid is a giant pile of stone with almost nothing in it that can wear out, break down or become obsolete. It lasts because it is massive and simple and sits in a dry desert. A pile of gravel will outlast a jet engine. That does not make the gravel pile the more advanced technology.

So I will ask it again, since you did not answer it the first time: more advanced at WHAT, precisely? Name the capability. Then show the evidence for it. "They built things and the things are still there" is circular - it just says they were good at building the things they built.
